{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794564749942,"sessionID":"ses_e2b9b776cffe9oHJ15dLLxNWNn","part":{"partID":"prt_1d464f699001TEOqm2bTgEPQfk","sessionID":"ses_e2b9b776cffe9oHJ15dLLxNWNn","messageID":"msg_1d464e2e9001n7djVxyz7ms4cB","type":"tool","id":"call_00_jLobwpgmUIiD2cHeBmnU7647","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-421\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n29: \n30: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n31: \n32: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n33: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n34: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n35: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n36: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n37: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n38: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n39: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n40: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '../../../../../..');\n41: const CODEX_CHECK = join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs');\n42: \n43: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n44:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n45: }\n46: \n47: /**\n48:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n49:  *\n50:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n51:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n52:  *\n53:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n54:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n55:  */\n56: export function parseOffer(head) {\n57:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n58:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n59:   if (!match) {\n60:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n61:   }\n62:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n63:   return {\n64:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n65:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n66:   };\n67: }\n68: \n69: /**\n70:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n71:  *\n72:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n73:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n74:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n75:  *\n76:  * @param {object} options\n77:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n78:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n79:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n80:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n81:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n82:  */\n83: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n84:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n85:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n86:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n87:   }\n88: \n89:   const payload = {\n90:     prompt,\n91:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n92:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n93:     session_id: sessionId,\n94:   };\n95: \n96:   let result;\n97:   try {\n98:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n99:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n100:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n101:       encoding: 'utf8',\n102:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n103:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n104:     });\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n107:   }\n108: \n109:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n110:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n111:   }\n112: \n113:   let output;\n114:   try {\n115:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n116:   } catch {\n117:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n118:   }\n119: \n120:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n121:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n122:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n123:   }\n124: \n125:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n126:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n127:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n131: }\n132: \n133: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n134:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n135:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n136:   return content.some((part) => {\n137:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n138:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n139:   });\n140: }\n141: \n142: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n143: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n144: // whichever block carries it.\n145: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n146:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n147:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n148: }\n149: \n150: // Injected context sits beside the records rather than inside them, so a reader\n151: // that reports it gives the brief a second place to be found.\n152: function injectedHasBrief(injected) {\n153:   return (Array.isArray(injected) ? injected : []).some(\n154:     (entry) => typeof entry?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(entry.text),\n155:   );\n156: }\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * Ask the project's Codex approval check whether its advisor hook can run.\n160:  *\n161:  * Codex skips an unapproved project hook without a warning, so a wired hook can\n162:  * still deliver nothing. The check reports the approval recorded in the config\n163:  * file it reads, and this wrapper turns that verdict into the answer the\n164:  * delivery decision needs.\n165:  *\n166:  * @param {object} [options]\n167:  * @param {string} [options.projectDir] - Project root whose hooks file is read.\n168:  * @param {string} [options.configPath] - Codex config file, defaulting to the\n169:  *   environment's Codex home.\n170:  * @returns {'approved' | 'unapproved' | 'absent' | 'no-hooks' | 'unknown'}\n171:  */\n172: export function codexHookApproval({ projectDir, configPath } = {}) {\n173:   if (typeof projectDir !== 'string' || projectDir === '') return 'unknown';\n174: \n175:   const args = [CODEX_CHECK, '--root', projectDir, '--json'];\n176:   if (configPath) args.push('--config', configPath);\n177:   const result = spawnSync('node', args, { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 10_000 });\n178:   if (result.error || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') return 'unknown';\n179: \n180:   let verdict;\n181:   try {\n182:     verdict = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n183:   } catch {\n184:     return 'unknown';\n185:   }\n186: \n187:   switch (verdict?.code) {\n188:     case 'codex-hooks-approved':\n189:       return 'approved';\n190:     case 'codex-hooks-unapproved':\n191:       return 'unapproved';\n192:     case 'codex-advisor-hook-absent':\n193:       return 'absent';\n194:     case 'no-codex-hooks':\n195:       return 'no-hooks';\n196:     default:\n197:       return 'unknown';\n198:   }\n199: }\n200: \n201: /**\n202:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n203:  *\n204:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n205:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi, Devin and DSH keep the brief in their\n206:  * session store, either as a record or as injected context beside it, and Codex\n207:  * answers from its approval plus whatever its store holds. OpenCode stays\n208:  * `unknown`, because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no\n209:  * copy.\n210:  *\n211:  * @param {object} options\n212:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n213:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n214:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n215:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n216:  * @param {string} [options.codexConfig] - Codex config file the approval is\n217:  *   read from, defaulting to the environment's Codex home.\n218:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n219:  */\n220: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession, codexConfig } = {}) {\n221:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n222:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n223:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n224:       return 'unknown';\n225:     }\n226: \n227:     let raw;\n228:     try {\n229:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n230:     } catch {\n231:       return 'unknown';\n232:     }\n233: \n234:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n235:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n236:       let entry;\n237:       try {\n238:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n239:       } catch {\n240:         continue;\n241:       }\n242:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n243:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n244:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n245:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n246:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n247:     }\n248:     return false;\n249:   }\n250: \n251:   if (runtime === 'pi' || runtime === 'devin' || runtime === 'dsh') {\n252:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n253:     let loaded;\n254:     try {\n255:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n256:     } catch {\n257:       return 'unknown';\n258:     }\n259:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n260:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) || injectedHasBrief(loaded.injected);\n261:   }\n262: \n263:   if (runtime === 'codex') {\n264:     // An unapproved project hook never runs, so the brief cannot have arrived.\n265:     // An approved one may store its context where no reader looks yet.\n266:     if (codexHookApproval({ projectDir: locator?.projectDir, configPath: codexConfig }) === 'unapproved') {\n267:       return false;\n268:     }\n269: \n270:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n271:     let loaded;\n272:     try {\n273:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n274:     } catch {\n275:       return 'unknown';\n276:     }\n277:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n278:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) || injectedHasBrief(loaded.injected)\n279:       ? true\n280:       : 'unknown';\n281:   }\n282: \n283:   return 'unknown';\n284: }\n285: \n286: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n287: // Run-log backfill\n288: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n289: \n290: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n291: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n292: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n293: // own error instead of losing the line.\n294: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n295:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n296:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n297:   spawnSync(\n298:     'node',\n299:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n300:     {\n301:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n302:       encoding: 'utf8',\n303:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n304:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n305:     },\n306:   );\n307: }\n308: \n309: function refused(code, detail) {\n310:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n311: }\n312: \n313: /**\n314:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n315:  *\n316:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n317:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n318:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n319:  * log is still written.\n320:  *\n321:  * @param {object} options\n322:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n323:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n324:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n325:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n326:  * @param {boolean} [options.briefOnly] - Write only the delivery answer and\n327:  *   leave every offer field exactly as the input line holds it.\n328:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n329:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n330:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n331:  *   injectable for tests.\n332:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n333:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n334:  */\n335: export async function backfillRunLog({\n336:   inPath,\n337:   outPath,\n338:   promptFor,\n339:   briefOnly = false,\n340:   replay = replayOffer,\n341:   brief = briefDelivered,\n342:   warm = warmCheckout,\n343: }) {\n344:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n345:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n346:   }\n347: \n348:   const rows = [];\n349:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n350:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n351:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n352:   }\n353: \n354:   const prompts = [];\n355:   for (const row of rows) {\n356:     try {\n357:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n358:     } catch (error) {\n359:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n360:     }\n361:   }\n362: \n363:   const warmed = new Set();\n364:   const output = [];\n365:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n366:     const line = rows[index];\n367:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n368:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n369:       ? line.locator\n370:       : null;\n371:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n372:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n373: \n374:     let offer = null;\n375:     if (!briefOnly) {\n376:       offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n377:       if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n378:         if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n379:           warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n380:           try {\n381:             await warm(checkoutDir);\n382:           } catch {\n383:             // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n384:           }\n385:         }\n386:         offer = await replay({\n387:           checkoutDir,\n388:           prompt: prompts[index],\n389:           sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n390:         });\n391:       }\n392:     }\n393: \n394:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n395:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n396:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n397:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n398: \n399:     // A recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw, so recomputing\n400:     // delivery must not replay it. A brief-only pass copies the line forward\n401:     // untouched, so a present offer stays and an absent one stays absent.\n402:     let nextLine;\n403:     if (briefOnly) {\n404:       nextLine = { ...line, briefDelivered: delivered, briefBackfilled: true };\n405:     } else {\n406:       nextLine = {\n407:         ...line,\n408:         offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n409:         offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n410:         offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n411:         briefDelivered: delivered,\n412:         offerBackfilled: true,\n413:       };\n414:     }\n415:     output.push(nextLine);\n416:   }\n417: \n418:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n419:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n420:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n421: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-421\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n29: \n30: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n31: \n32: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n33: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n34: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n35: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n36: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n37: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n38: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n39: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n40: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '../../../../../..');\n41: const CODEX_CHECK = join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs');\n42: \n43: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n44:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n45: }\n46: \n47: /**\n48:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n49:  *\n50:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n51:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n52:  *\n53:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n54:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n55:  */\n56: export function parseOffer(head) {\n57:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n58:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n59:   if (!match) {\n60:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n61:   }\n62:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n63:   return {\n64:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n65:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n66:   };\n67: }\n68: \n69: /**\n70:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n71:  *\n72:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n73:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n74:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n75:  *\n76:  * @param {object} options\n77:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n78:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n79:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n80:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n81:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n82:  */\n83: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n84:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n85:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n86:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n87:   }\n88: \n89:   const payload = {\n90:     prompt,\n91:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n92:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n93:     session_id: sessionId,\n94:   };\n95: \n96:   let result;\n97:   try {\n98:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n99:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n100:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n101:       encoding: 'utf8',\n102:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n103:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n104:     });\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n107:   }\n108: \n109:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n110:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n111:   }\n112: \n113:   let output;\n114:   try {\n115:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n116:   } catch {\n117:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n118:   }\n119: \n120:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n121:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n122:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n123:   }\n124: \n125:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n126:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n127:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n131: }\n132: \n133: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n134:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n135:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n136:   return content.some((part) => {\n137:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n138:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n139:   });\n140: }\n141: \n142: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n143: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n144: // whichever block carries it.\n145: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n146:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n147:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n148: }\n149: \n150: // Injected context sits beside the records rather than inside them, so a reader\n151: // that reports it gives the brief a second place to be found.\n152: function injectedHasBrief(injected) {\n153:   return (Array.isArray(injected) ? injected : []).some(\n154:     (entry) => typeof entry?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(entry.text),\n155:   );\n156: }\n157: \n158: /**\n159:  * Ask the project's Codex approval check whether its advisor hook can run.\n160:  *\n161:  * Codex skips an unapproved project hook without a warning, so a wired hook can\n162:  * still deliver nothing. The check reports the approval recorded in the config\n163:  * file it reads, and this wrapper turns that verdict into the answer the\n164:  * delivery decision needs.\n165:  *\n166:  * @param {object} [options]\n167:  * @param {string} [options.projectDir] - Project root whose hooks file is read.\n168:  * @param {string} [options.configPath] - Codex config file, defaulting to the\n169:  *   environment's Codex home.\n170:  * @returns {'approved' | 'unapproved' | 'absent' | 'no-hooks' | 'unknown'}\n171:  */\n172: export function codexHookApproval({ projectDir, configPath } = {}) {\n173:   if (typeof projectDir !== 'string' || projectDir === '') return 'unknown';\n174: \n175:   const args = [CODEX_CHECK, '--root', projectDir, '--json'];\n176:   if (configPath) args.push('--config', configPath);\n177:   const result = spawnSync('node', args, { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 10_000 });\n178:   if (result.error || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') return 'unknown';\n179: \n180:   let verdict;\n181:   try {\n182:     verdict = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n183:   } catch {\n184:     return 'unknown';\n185:   }\n186: \n187:   switch (verdict?.code) {\n188:     case 'codex-hooks-approved':\n189:       return 'approved';\n190:     case 'codex-hooks-unapproved':\n191:       return 'unapproved';\n192:     case 'codex-advisor-hook-absent':\n193:       return 'absent';\n194:     case 'no-codex-hooks':\n195:       return 'no-hooks';\n196:     default:\n197:       return 'unknown';\n198:   }\n199: }\n200: \n201: /**\n202:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n203:  *\n204:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n205:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi, Devin and DSH keep the brief in their\n206:  * session store, either as a record or as injected context beside it, and Codex\n207:  * answers from its approval plus whatever its store holds. OpenCode stays\n208:  * `unknown`, because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no\n209:  * copy.\n210:  *\n211:  * @param {object} options\n212:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n213:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n214:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n215:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n216:  * @param {string} [options.codexConfig] - Codex config file the approval is\n217:  *   read from, defaulting to the environment's Codex home.\n218:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n219:  */\n220: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession, codexConfig } = {}) {\n221:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n222:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n223:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n224:       return 'unknown';\n225:     }\n226: \n227:     let raw;\n228:     try {\n229:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n230:     } catch {\n231:       return 'unknown';\n232:     }\n233: \n234:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n235:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n236:       let entry;\n237:       try {\n238:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n239:       } catch {\n240:         continue;\n241:       }\n242:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n243:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n244:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n245:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n246:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n247:     }\n248:     return false;\n249:   }\n250: \n251:   if (runtime === 'pi' || runtime === 'devin' || runtime === 'dsh') {\n252:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n253:     let loaded;\n254:     try {\n255:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n256:     } catch {\n257:       return 'unknown';\n258:     }\n259:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n260:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) || injectedHasBrief(loaded.injected);\n261:   }\n262: \n263:   if (runtime === 'codex') {\n264:     // An unapproved project hook never runs, so the brief cannot have arrived.\n265:     // An approved one may store its context where no reader looks yet.\n266:     if (codexHookApproval({ projectDir: locator?.projectDir, configPath: codexConfig }) === 'unapproved') {\n267:       return false;\n268:     }\n269: \n270:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n271:     let loaded;\n272:     try {\n273:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n274:     } catch {\n275:       return 'unknown';\n276:     }\n277:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n278:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) || injectedHasBrief(loaded.injected)\n279:       ? true\n280:       : 'unknown';\n281:   }\n282: \n283:   return 'unknown';\n284: }\n285: \n286: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n287: // Run-log backfill\n288: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n289: \n290: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n291: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n292: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n293: // own error instead of losing the line.\n294: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n295:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n296:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n297:   spawnSync(\n298:     'node',\n299:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n300:     {\n301:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n302:       encoding: 'utf8',\n303:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n304:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n305:     },\n306:   );\n307: }\n308: \n309: function refused(code, detail) {\n310:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n311: }\n312: \n313: /**\n314:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n315:  *\n316:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n317:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n318:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n319:  * log is still written.\n320:  *\n321:  * @param {object} options\n322:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n323:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n324:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n325:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n326:  * @param {boolean} [options.briefOnly] - Write only the delivery answer and\n327:  *   leave every offer field exactly as the input line holds it.\n328:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n329:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n330:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n331:  *   injectable for tests.\n332:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n333:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n334:  */\n335: export async function backfillRunLog({\n336:   inPath,\n337:   outPath,\n338:   promptFor,\n339:   briefOnly = false,\n340:   replay = replayOffer,\n341:   brief = briefDelivered,\n342:   warm = warmCheckout,\n343: }) {\n344:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n345:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n346:   }\n347: \n348:   const rows = [];\n349:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n350:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n351:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n352:   }\n353: \n354:   const prompts = [];\n355:   for (const row of rows) {\n356:     try {\n357:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n358:     } catch (error) {\n359:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n360:     }\n361:   }\n362: \n363:   const warmed = new Set();\n364:   const output = [];\n365:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n366:     const line = rows[index];\n367:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n368:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n369:       ? line.locator\n370:       : null;\n371:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n372:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n373: \n374:     let offer = null;\n375:     if (!briefOnly) {\n376:       offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n377:       if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n378:         if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n379:           warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n380:           try {\n381:             await warm(checkoutDir);\n382:           } catch {\n383:             // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n384:           }\n385:         }\n386:         offer = await replay({\n387:           checkoutDir,\n388:           prompt: prompts[index],\n389:           sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n390:         });\n391:       }\n392:     }\n393: \n394:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n395:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n396:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n397:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n398: \n399:     // A recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw, so recomputing\n400:     // delivery must not replay it. A brief-only pass copies the line forward\n401:     // untouched, so a present offer stays and an absent one stays absent.\n402:     let nextLine;\n403:     if (briefOnly) {\n404:       nextLine = { ...line, briefDelivered: delivered, briefBackfilled: true };\n405:     } else {\n406:       nextLine = {\n407:         ...line,\n408:         offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n409:         offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n410:         offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n411:         briefDelivered: delivered,\n412:         offerBackfilled: true,\n413:       };\n414:     }\n415:     output.push(nextLine);\n416:   }\n417: \n418:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n419:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n420:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n421: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794564748953,"end":1794564749942}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794564749958,"sessionID":"ses_e2b9b776cffe9oHJ15dLLxNWNn","part":{"partID":"prt_1d464f91f001BdyPefj71ANeDS","sessionID":"ses_e2b9b776cffe9oHJ15dLLxNWNn","messageID":"msg_1d464e2e9001n7djVxyz7ms4cB","type":"tool","id":"call_02_IyYpXnKEFeXyDT3HdsjH6971","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls REPO\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null && sed -n '1,120p' \"REPO RULES.md\"","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"REPO RULES.md\n# REPO RULES\n\nRepo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\nbefore your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\nfile that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\nholds no rules of its own.\n\n---\n\n## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n\n1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n   specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n   edge case.\n5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n\n### Precedence\n\n| Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n|-------|--------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n| 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n| 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n| 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n\nA rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n`AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\nwhile what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\nis not.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n| You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n|-------------------|------|-----------|\n| Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n| Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n| Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n| Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n| Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n| Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n| Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n| Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n| End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n| Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n\n---\n\n## 3. INDEX\n\n| Rule | Summary |\n|------|---------|\n| [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n| [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n| [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n| [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n| [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n| [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n| [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n| [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n| [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n| [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n\nEach file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\npermit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n\n**In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\nthe posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\nreads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\nback to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\nsecond, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\ntrigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\nwhich fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\nthe two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n\n**Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\nof agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\nThose belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\nabsent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\nposture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n\n**The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\nroute stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\nIn; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\nthe mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\nadmits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n\n**The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\noverrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\nnaming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\nabove pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\nand what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\nruntimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\nasks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\na selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\nwhich of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\nit names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\nruntimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"REPO RULES.md\n# REPO RULES\n\nRepo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\nbefore your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\nfile that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\nholds no rules of its own.\n\n---\n\n## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n\n1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n   specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n   edge case.\n5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n\n### Precedence\n\n| Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n|-------|--------|--------------------|\n| 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n| 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n| 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n| 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n\nA rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n`AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\nwhile what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\nis not.\n\n---\n\n## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n| You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n|-------------------|------|-----------|\n| Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n| Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n| Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n| Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n| Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n| Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n| Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n| Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n| End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n| Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n\n---\n\n## 3. INDEX\n\n| Rule | Summary |\n|------|---------|\n| [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n| [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n| [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n| [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n| [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n| [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n| [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n| [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n| [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n| [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n\nEach file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\npermit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n\n**In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\nthe posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\nreads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\nback to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\nsecond, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\ntrigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\nwhich fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\nthe two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n\n**Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\nof agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\nThose belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\nabsent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\nposture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n\n**The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\nroute stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\nIn; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\nthe mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\nadmits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n\n**The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\noverrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\nnaming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\nabove pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\nand what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\nruntimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\nasks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\na selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\nwhich of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\nis the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\nit names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\nruntimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"ls REPO\\\\ RULES.md 2>/dev/null && sed -n '1,120p' \\\"REPO RULES.md\\\"\", \"workdir\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794564749599,"end":1794564749958}}}}
